User-defined function to calculate fine based on overdue days (Rs. 50 per day). SQL Server example:
```sql
CREATE FUNCTION dbo.CalcOverdueFine (@LoanID INT)
RETURNS DECIMAL(10,2)
AS
BEGIN
    DECLARE @OverdueDays INT;
    DECLARE @Fine DECIMAL(10,2) = 0;

    SELECT @OverdueDays = DATEDIFF(DAY, DueDate, GETDATE())
    FROM Loan
    WHERE LoanID = @LoanID AND ReturnDate IS NULL;

    IF @OverdueDays > 0
        SET @Fine = @OverdueDays * 50.00;

    RETURN @Fine;
END;
```
Usage: SELECT dbo.CalcOverdueFine(101) AS FineAmount;

Authentication in SQL Server: process of validating user identity. Two modes: Windows Authentication (uses Active Directory credentials) and SQL Server Authentication (username/password stored in SQL Server). Authorization: granting permissions to authenticated principals on securables (databases, tables, schemas, procedures). SQL Server implements role-based access control (RBAC) using fixed server roles (e.g., sysadmin, securityadmin, serveradmin) and fixed database roles (db_owner, db_securityadmin, db_accessadmin, db_backupoperator, db_datareader, db_datawriter, db_ddladmin). Custom roles can also be created. T-SQL statements to create login and assign roles:
```sql
-- Create SQL Server login
CREATE LOGIN library_admin WITH PASSWORD = 'Lib@2021!', DEFAULT_DATABASE = LibraryDB;
-- Create user for the login in the database
USE LibraryDB;
CREATE USER library_admin FOR LOGIN library_admin;
-- Add user to fixed database role db_owner
EXEC sp_addrolemember 'db_owner', 'library_admin';
-- Alternatively, create custom role and grant permissions
CREATE ROLE loan_processor;
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON Loan TO loan_processor;
CREATE USER loan_clerk FOR LOGIN loan_clerk_login;
EXEC sp_addrolemember 'loan_processor', 'loan_clerk';
-- Grant execute permission on function
GRANT EXECUTE ON dbo.CalcOverdueFine TO loan_processor;
```
Also use REVOKE and DENY to restrict permissions.
